DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammddiiccee reads a PAM, PBM, PGM, or PPM image as input and splits it hor‐
       izontally and/or vertically into equal size pieces and writes them into
       separate  files as the same kind of image.  You can optionally make the
       pieces overlap.

       See the --oouuttsstteemm option for information on naming of the output files.

       The --wwiiddtthh and --hheeiigghhtt options determine the size of the output pieces.

       ppaammuunnddiiccee can rejoin the images.  For finer control, you can also use

       ppnnmmccaatt.

       One use for this is to make pieces that take  less  computer  resources
       than  the whole image to process.  For example, you might have an image
       so large that an image editor can't read it all  into  memory  or  pro‐
       cesses  it  very  slowly.   With ppaammddiiccee, you can split it into smaller
       pieces, edit one at a time, and then reassemble them.

       Another use for this is to print a large image in  small  printer-sized
       pieces  that  you can glue together.  ppppmmgglloobbee does a similar thing; it
       lets you glue the pieces together into a sphere.

       If you want to cut pieces from an image individually, not in a  regular
       grid, use ppaammccuutt.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ),  ppaammddiiccee  recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --oouuttsstteemm==filenamestem
              This option determines the names of the output files.  Each out‐
              put file is named _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e_s_t_e_m___y___x.._t_y_p_e  where  _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e_s_t_e_m  is
              the value of the --oouuttsstteemm option, _x and y are the horizontal and
              vertical locations, respectively, in the input image of the out‐
              put  image,  zero  being the leftmost and top, and _t_y_p_e is ..ppbbmm,
              ..ppggmm, ..ppppmm, or ..ppaamm, depending on the type of image.


       --wwiiddtthh==_w_i_d_t_h
              gives the width in pixels of the output images.   The  rightmost
              pieces  are smaller than this if the input image is not a multi‐
              ple of _w_i_d_t_h pixels wide.


       --hheeiigghhtt==_h_e_i_g_h_t
              gives the height in pixels of the  output  images.   The  bottom
              pieces  are smaller than this if the input image is not a multi‐
              ple of _h_e_i_g_h_t pixels high.


       --hhoovveerrllaapp==_h_o_v_e_r_l_a_p
              gives the horizontal overlap in pixels  between  output  images.
              Each  image  in  a row will overlap the previous one by _h_o_v_e_r_l_a_p
              pixels.  By default, there is no overlap.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004).


       --vvoovveerrllaapp==_v_o_v_e_r_l_a_p
              gives the vertical overlap  in  pixels  between  output  images.
              Each  row  of  images  will overlap the previous row by _v_o_v_e_r_l_a_p
              pixels.  By default, there is no overlap.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004).


       --vveerrbboossee
              Print information about the processing to Standard Error.




HHIISSTTOORRYY
       ppaammddiiccee was new in Netpbm 9.25 (March 2002).

       Before Netpbm 10.29 (August 2005), there was a limit of 100  slices  in
       each direction.


